    <section class="bio">
        <p>I care more about how software gets built than what it does. Every project on the home page is really the same handful of ideas — test-first, minimal dependencies, first principles over frameworks — applied to a different problem.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="philosophy">
        <h2>Philosophy</h2>
        <div class="domain-group">
            <ul>
                <li><strong>TDD First</strong>: London style — test against interfaces, test behavior, mocks as DRYed fakes. Non-negotiable across every language and project, not a phase I grew out of.</li>
                <li><strong>Minimalism</strong>: Reach for the smaller dependency — Helidon SE over Spring Boot, vanilla JS over React, Void Linux over Ubuntu. Every dependency is a maintenance liability I have to justify keeping.</li>
                <li><strong>First Principles</strong>: Understanding beats memorizing an API. I've written OOP in pure C to see how the pattern actually works, built a TCP server in Zig, put together a custom Linux distro from scratch.</li>
                <li><strong>Simplicity Over Cleverness</strong>: Readable code beats clever code. I distrust magic — reflection, hidden containers, framework bloat — it's always the first thing that breaks at 2am.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

